   3 ## The biped's walking gait (walker-feel, 18 Nov 2026): foot paths and solved legs. State only; `TLRigBiped` owns one,
   4 ## steps it once a tick and asks it for the pelvis and leg pose. Nothing here reads a clock or a random function.
   5 ## The gait is foot paths; the legs are solved (two bones, exact). A stance foot's anchor (where its ankle stands when the
   6 ## boot is flat, in model x and z) moves each tick by exactly minus the body's velocity, so the sole stands still on the
   7 ## floor at any speed, direction and acceleration. Cadence (cycles per second), duty (the share of a cycle a foot is down),
   8 ## lift, crouch and bounce are keyed by the speed; the stride follows from them (stride = duty x speed / cadence). The
   9 ## pelvis rides as high as the style asks and no higher than both legs can reach, so a walk vaults over the standing leg.
  10 ## Sidestep and backward: the feet travel along the real direction of `velocity_local`; the pelvis turns 0.6 of the way
  11 ## towards it (the chest turns back, so he keeps facing forward); past about 100 degrees he backpedals.
  12 const LEG: float = 0.42          # each leg segment, metres (hip to knee, knee to ankle)
  13 const LEG_MAX: float = 0.832     # the furthest the ankle goes from the hip joint (a locked knee reads as a stick)
  14 const ANKLE_H: float = 0.10      # the ankle above the sole
  15 const _G_V: Array = [0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.4, 2.0, 2.6, 3.5, 4.5, 6.0, 9.0, 12.0]            # speed keys, m/s at scale 1
  16 const _G_F: Array = [0.70, 0.75, 1.00, 1.18, 1.45, 1.50, 1.50, 1.52, 1.75, 2.35, 2.70]   # cadence, cycles/s (two steps)
  17 const _G_D: Array = [0.66, 0.66, 0.64, 0.62, 0.56, 0.44, 0.36, 0.30, 0.26, 0.215, 0.18]  # duty: under 0.5 = a flight phase
